<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/"><channel><title>Junbo Wang</title><link>https://junbowang.com/</link><description>Recent content on Junbo Wang</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><lastBuildDate>Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://junbowang.com/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Two books about AI</title><link>https://junbowang.com/posts/review_1/</link><pubDate>Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://junbowang.com/posts/review_1/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="klara-and-the-sun"&gt;Klara and the Sun&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p class="byline"&gt;Kazuo Ishiguro · 2021&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure class="sq"&gt;
&lt;img class="sq-bg" src="Klara_and_the_Sun.png" alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;
&lt;img class="sq-fg" src="Klara_and_the_Sun.png" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;a href="https://www.goodreads.com/book/show/54120408"&gt;Goodreads&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A fully conscientious robot with the mind of a child and yet superhuman abilities being sold as an Artificial Friend for children. I love that you could not really tell what Ishiguro intended the message of the story to be. Most of the story revolves around how a cognitively unevenly (or compared to humans) developed robot sees and learns about the world. The choice that the AFs are fully equipped top-of-the-spec shells for near zero understanding of the human world makes it less a story about robots but one about how humans always end up disappointing each other and how we always eventually grow up and move on. All Klara had left in the end was the benevolent Sun.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Two excruciating works</title><link>https://junbowang.com/posts/review_2/</link><pubDate>Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://junbowang.com/posts/review_2/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="husbands"&gt;Husbands&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure class="sq"&gt;
&lt;img class="sq-bg" src="Husbands.jpg" alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;
&lt;img class="sq-fg" src="Husbands.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async"&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;a href="https://letterboxd.com/film/husbands/"&gt;Letterboxd&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I saw Husbands by John Cassavetes at Bergen Filmklubb. Excruciating is the only word I have to describe it. You follow three middle-aged white collar bros in New York getting shitfaced after attending a bro’s funeral. Recognizing their mortality, they decide to go on the ultimate bro’s trip. Basketball. Singing party with senior citizens. Beating up the wife and terrorizing the family. Trying to puke in the toilet but your bro is talking too much. Flying to London with no luggage. Losing 200 pounds in one game at a London casino. Trying to hook up with women but all you are doing is either crying in bed together or attempting (roleplaying?) rape. The ultimate frat bro movie but with middle-aged guys.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Tobias's tab</title><link>https://junbowang.com/posts/tobias-tab/</link><pubDate>Sat, 25 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://junbowang.com/posts/tobias-tab/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="in-which-i-keep-track-of-how-much-money-i-owe-mr-tobias-eiksund"&gt;in which i keep track of how much money i owe mr. tobias eiksund&lt;/h1&gt;
